+// cranvr: creates and destroys shared Direct3D 11 textures and fences many times, and opens each of them in a second
+// process, as SteamVR, its web helper and a game do. It tests what DXMT leaves in launchd: before cranvr's DXMT
+// patch 0020, each shared texture of DXMT's stock path and each shared fence put a port under a name in launchd, and
+// launchd kept the port and the memory behind it until the user logged out.
+//
+// Each cycle makes these objects, and the second process (the same program with --child) opens each one, checks its
+// description and, for a color texture, the color that the first process cleared it to:
+//   rgba16f      R16G16B16A16_FLOAT, legacy shared handle (stock path: the format)
+//   bgra8        B8G8R8A8_UNORM, legacy shared handle (stock path: a legacy handle, as SteamVR's textures have)
+//   rgba8-mips   R8G8B8A8_UNORM with 4 mip levels, legacy shared handle (stock path: mipmaps)
+//   bgra8-array  B8G8R8A8_UNORM with 2 slices, NT handle (stock path: slices); the check reads slice 1
+//   rgba8-msaa   R8G8B8A8_UNORM with 4 samples, legacy shared handle (stock path: MSAA); no color check
+//   keyed        R8G8B8A8_UNORM with a keyed mutex, legacy shared handle (stock path, and the keyed mutex's event)
+//   iosurface    B8G8R8A8_UNORM, NT handle (cranvr's IOSurface path of DXMT patch 0019, by the surface's ID)
+//   fence        a shared ID3D11Fence, NT handle: the first process signals 1, the second waits for it and signals 2,
+//                and the first waits for 2. In Wine 11.10 the second process cannot open it: DXMT's OpenSharedFence
+//                reads the fence's private data with D3DKMTQueryResourceInfoFromNtHandle, which Wine answers for
+//                resources only, and DXMT's CreateFence never stores the data (Wine's D3DKMT_ESCAPE_UPDATE_RESOURCE_WINE
+//                takes resources only too, and DXMT's check of it is the wrong way round). The program then counts
+//                the cycles whose fence did not open and goes on; the first process still makes and shares the
+//                fence, which put a name in launchd before DXMT patch 0020.
+// The first process then releases everything and starts the next cycle. The second releases what it opened at the
+// end of each cycle.
+//
+// At the end the first process tests the size check of the IOSurface path: it shares an IOSurface texture, releases
+// it, and makes IOSurface textures of another width until macOS gives one of them the ID of the released surface
+// (macOS reuses the IDs of freed surfaces at once). The second process must then refuse to open the handle of the
+// released texture, because its ID names a surface of another size now.
+//
+// Usage: shared_churn.exe [--cycles N] [--width W] [--height H] [--signal FILE]
+// The textures are W x H (default 640 x 333). With --signal FILE the program stops three times, so that a script can
+// count the names in launchd and measure memory while both processes live. In its first cycle, while both processes
+// hold the objects, it prints "shared_churn: measure live" and waits for FILE.live to exist; after that cycle it
+// prints "shared_churn: measure warm" and waits for FILE.warm; after its last cycle it prints "shared_churn: measure
+// churned" and waits for FILE.churned. It waits at most 30 s each time.
+//
+// It prints "shared_churn: PASS" with the number of cycles and the time that one took, or "shared_churn: FAIL ...".
+
+#include <windows.h>
+
+#include <d3d11_4.h>
+#include <dxgi1_2.h>
+
+#include <chrono>
+#include <cstdint>
+#include <cstdio>
+#include <cstdlib>
+#include <cstring>
+#include <string>
+#include <thread>
+#include <vector>
